Set renderer.toneMapping = ACESFilmicToneMapping, exposure 1.0. Scene renders into HalfFloat target; does material toneMapping apply when rendering to target? In three, tone mapping is applied in material fragment only when renderer.toneMapping != NoToneMapping, regardless of target. Then OutputPass would tone map again → washed out. Standard three examples with EffectComposer set renderer.toneMapping = NoToneMapping? Let's recall: Since r152, WebGLRenderer tone mapping applies only when render target is null? Actually in WebGLProgram, toneMapping is included if material.toneMapped && renderer.toneMapping != NoToneMapping; renderer sets `toneMapping` define from renderer.toneMapping for all renders. The OutputPass is recommended and examples set renderer.toneMapping in constructor? Many official examples (e.g., webgl_postprocessing_unreal_bloom) do:
```
renderer.toneMapping = THREE.ReinhardToneMapping;
...
const outputPass = new OutputPass();
composer.addPass(outputPass);
```
And it looks okay because RenderPass renders to composer target with tone mapping applied? If applied twice it would be visibly washed. Hmm, I think in recent three, tone mapping is only applied when rendering to screen: WebGLRenderer sets `_currentRenderTarget === null ? toneMapping : NoToneMapping` for materials. Let me check three source quickly: search "toneMapping" in WebGLRenderer/WebGLPrograms.

