import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join } from 'node:path';
const runtime = join(process.env.HOME, '.local/share/omgithub-playwright');
const require = createRequire(join(runtime, 'package.json'));
const { chromium } = require('playwright');
const config = JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(runtime, 'linux.json'), 'utf8'));
process.env.DISPLAY ||= ':' + readFileSync(join(runtime, 'display'), 'utf8').trim();
const browser = await chromium.launch({ ...config.browser.launchOptions, timeout: 30000 });
const page = await browser.newPage({ viewport: { width: 1280, height: 800 } });
page.on('pageerror', e => console.log('PAGEERROR:', e.message));
await page.goto('http://localhost:3002/', { waitUntil: 'load', timeout: 60000 });
await page.waitForTimeout(4000);
await page.getByLabel('Play Now').click();
await page.waitForTimeout(5000);
await page.evaluate(() => {
  const g = window.__nightfall.game;
  g.player.godMode = true; g.enemies.reset();
  g.pendingWave = 0; g.waveActive = false; g.intermission = 999;
  g.player.position.set(0, 0, 17); g.player.yaw = 0; g.player.pitch = 0;
  document.querySelectorAll('.wave-banner,.boss-bar,.notice').forEach(e => e.style.display = 'none');
});
await page.waitForTimeout(800);
await page.screenshot({ path: '/tmp/opencode/shots/w-hip.jpg', type: 'jpeg', quality: 88 });
await page.evaluate(() => { window.__nightfall.game.input.mouse.right = true; });
await page.waitForTimeout(1300);
await page.screenshot({ path: '/tmp/opencode/shots/w-ads.jpg', type: 'jpeg', quality: 88 });
await page.evaluate(() => { const g = window.__nightfall.game; g.input.mouse.right = false; g.weapons.switchImmediate('pistol'); });
await page.waitForTimeout(700);
await page.screenshot({ path: '/tmp/opencode/shots/w-pistol.jpg', type: 'jpeg', quality: 88 });
await page.evaluate(() => { window.__nightfall.game.input.mouse.right = true; });
await page.waitForTimeout(1200);
await page.screenshot({ path: '/tmp/opencode/shots/w-pistolads.jpg', type: 'jpeg', quality: 88 });
await browser.close();

content.text:
Now I see clearly:

Hip (w-hip): The rifle looks good-ish (dark navy) and is positioned bottom center-right. But the model is too blocky and the entire front handguard merges with the receiver; missing clear barrel lines? It's acceptable but could be improved. The player is at z=17 looking at the blastwall at z=4.6; there are no enemies.

ADS (w-ads): The weapon is at center, but it is NOT aligned: the "sight" appears as a cyan rectangle at (510,300) while the center crosshair is at 640,400. Wait, the gun body is now centered around x≈535! It's offset LEFT of center. That's because the holo sight is at x=0 in holder, but holder x=0. How can the gun be left of center? The camera is at x=0 looking down -Z; holder at x=0. The gun geometry is symmetric around x=0 except the hands/sleeve offset. So the gun should be centered at 640. But it appears x≈530. Something's off.

Oh! The weapon shown in the center is an ENEMY? No, enemies reset. It's the rifle: dark rectangular body with a holo sight = cyan square. But the x position... Actually the image is 1024 wide? Wait, screenshots are 1280 wide but the rendering shown above appears 1024x640? Since we set viewport 1280x800; the image displayed may be scaled to 1024x640 by the viewer. So center x=512 in displayed image! The gun is at center x≈512 indeed. Crosshair center would be 512,400 (displayed) i.e., the holo sight at (510,300) is only 100px above center in displayed => 125px at 1280x800? Hmm displayed coordinates scale by 0.8: center y=320 in 640-high display. The cyan sight at y≈300 is 20px above center, which corresponds to 25px at 800 → the sight dot is slightly above center. The gun body extends below. That's actually correct ADS! Great. The weapon looks centered and the reticle/cyan glass at just above center. The dark slab below center is the receiver/stock, fine. It's still a bit plain but usable. The WARDEN banner is gone. Good.

However, the ADS weapon body is quite thick and pure black, and the sight window is a bright cyan rectangle instead of showing the reticle. The reticle sprite might be behind the glass plane and not visible because the glass additive cyan is brighter. Also the gun is too dark. The key light is from above right, but in ADS the gun is lit? It appears nearly black because view lights are farther and the gun's top surfaces face up. The model needs more light in ADS. Also the cyan glass is too bright/opaque (still) and looks like a screen. Reduce glass opacity to 0.03 and make the reticle more prominent. Hmm, it's at least visible.

Also note the rifle hip is still low-quality: it's a black mass, and the "Ammo" HUD overlaps the stock. In modern CoD, the gun has distinct details and textured surfaces. We can improve:
- Add subtle lighter edge highlights on the gun (different materials for rails) and a tan handguard is now dark brown, not visible? In w-hip, the whole gun is navy-black. The handguard tan color 0x766445 should look brown but appears dark gray-blue due to cool light; maybe our key light isn't reaching due to distance/position. The key at (1.05,0.85,0.25) with intensity 2.2 and distance 3.2. Distance to gun ~1.2 => 2.2/1.44 = 1.5, decent, but holder is scaled 0.64, gun's near parts at 0.5m from camera; key is maybe 1.3m from those → 1.3. Should light it. Maybe the materials' metalness 0.8 makes them dark (no env map). The gun metal is metalness 0.82 with no envMap → PBR metal without env is black except direct specular. That's why it looks black! Use lower metalness (0.4) and roughness 0.5, or add a scene environment map. Same for world metals (matMetal metalness 0.88) - they look dark too. A cheap env map from PMREM of the sky would dramatically improve metal materials. We have a generated sky; could render a cube render target once. Or set scene.environment to a simple gradient env from `RoomEnvironment`? That's an addon; we'd need to vendor RoomEnvironment and PMREMGenerator. Simpler: lower metalness for weapon materials and give gun materials some emissive. For world metal, lower metalness to ~0.55. But the wet ground uses metalness 0.24-0.92 and envMapIntensity; without env, puddles are black. They do look black (the "puddles" visible as dark patches). Adding an environment map would make everything reflect the sky and look much better.

Let's implement a procedural environment map using PMREMGenerator + a simple scene with sky gradient? Three.js PMREMGenerator.fromScene requires a scene; can use `new THREE.Scene()` with a hemisphere of colors? There's `THREE.PMREMGenerator.fromScene(scene, sigma, near, far)`; we can create a small scene with a large sphere using MeshBasicMaterial vertex colors or a gradient shader (BackSide). Or use the existing sky mesh: `pmrem.fromScene(this.scene)` would include all geometry — expensive and wrong (but only once). Better: build a mini scene with a giant sphere using the same sky shader (or a DataTexture equirect). Simplest: create an equirectangular canvas texture for the environment (night sky gradient with city glow) and use PMREMGenerator.fromEquirectangular. Then set scene.environment = envRT.texture. This will make metals and puddles reflect a believable sky.

We need to access renderer in World (it has renderer passed). Yes constructor World(scene, renderer). We can build after textures.

